524
00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:19.840
Bob Odenkirk was one of the Michaels, which is so crazy because I guess he was available as well since Slice O' Life didn't go forward.

525
00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:21.840
And Ken Zmanack was available.

526
00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:22.480
Yeah.

527
00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:22.900
Wow.

528
00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:25.680
We really got a lot of folks from Slice O' Life.

529
00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:26.660
We got lucky there.

530
00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:27.500
We scored.

531
00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:30.300
But Rain, I remember being paired with you.

532
00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:34.380
And I mean, I couldn't believe it.

533
00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:41.660
Of all of us, of all of us, you were 100% there from the beginning.

534
00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:46.080
I feel like from that moment that I did that first audition scene with you.

535
00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:49.140
Everybody else, I was paired with John.

536
00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:51.180
And I was like, oh, yeah, this is great.

537
00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:53.180
And Steve and everybody.

538
00:21:53.180 --> 00:22:01.580
But Rain, you were just like, I didn't even know where Rain began and Dwight ended or Dwight began and Rain ended.

539
00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:02.860
I mean, it was so fluid.

540
00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:04.240
It was amazing.

541
00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:08.400
But you were, I have to say, there's a mutual admiration society.

542
00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:12.880
For those who don't know, Jenna was totally in Pam, dressed as Pam.

543
00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:19.240
You were reading the book on John Belushi, Wired, that you thought Pam would be reading.

544
00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:21.640
It was in the script that she was reading it.

545
00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:22.980
So I read the whole novel.

546
00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:24.520
I read the whole thing.

547
00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:26.660
I was like, my character read it.

548
00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:27.360
I'll read it.

549
00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:28.880
Yeah.

550
00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:32.660
And you were Pam in the waiting room, too.

551
00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:35.660
But that was so much fun.

552
00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:46.140
And we had, I don't know if they'd ever released that audition, but that improvisation where I was just sitting, I was kneeling like way too close to you.

553
00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:46.520
Yeah.

554
00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:52.560
Like, which is something I love to do as Dwight is like, he doesn't have an understanding of like, personal space.

555
00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:53.400
He does not.

556
00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:56.580
He could be talking to someone like four inches away from them.

557
00:22:56.780 --> 00:22:58.640
And I thought, I always thought that was really funny.

558
00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:04.260
And where I talked about my girlfriend being stationed in Kuwait.

559
00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:08.860
And that was very funny.

560
00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:13.340
And you just, and it was such a great improv because most actors feel like, oh, I'm improv-ing.

561
00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:14.960
I need to talk a lot.

562
00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:18.920
You know, they think like, oh, improvisation means talking a lot.

563
00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:31.560
And this was an improv where I kind of did this monologue and you just gazed painfully into space, taking in my body odor and my proximity and the strangeness of my story.

564
00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:33.780
And it was, it was fantastic.

565
00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:37.000
And when we did that, I was like, oh, I know that we're going to have these parts.

566
00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:37.940
Yeah.

567
00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:39.180
I felt that way, too.

568
00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:43.300
And I felt at that audition, it was very clear to me that you were their choice.

569
00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:46.800
Did they keep pairing different people with Rain?

570
00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:47.900
Because that's how you know.

571
00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:52.580
You know that's the person when they're like, oh, let's see him with this person and this person and this person.

572
00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:54.480
And I only got paired with him once.

573
00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:59.080
And that made me a little nervous because it was very clear to me that Rain had the part.

574
00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:02.060
And so I was like, oh, well, I'll pair with him again.

575
00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:04.280
Yeah, they mostly matched me with a lot of different gyMs.

576
00:24:04.460 --> 00:24:06.420
I remember reading with a lot of different gyMs.

577
00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:06.720
Yeah.

578
00:24:07.460 --> 00:24:16.960
And the reason they said ultimately they went with John, I mean, obviously he's charming and handsome and funny and great deadpan and smart as hell.

579
00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:18.240
And like a ball.

580
00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:21.880
But that his size was comparable to mine.

581
00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:24.720
There were a couple other gyms that were like five foot seven.

582
00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:28.360
And it just seemed like Dwight would just overpower them.

583
00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:30.300
The gym was actually taller than me.

584
00:24:30.500 --> 00:24:34.580
So we kind of like we really matched up kind of energy wise.

585
00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:36.600
That makes sense to me.

586
00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:37.720
Yeah, that does.

587
00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:43.060
But then they went a whole other way with your romantic life and they picked the tiniest person in the office.

588
00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:46.740
Tiniest, tiniest little sprig.

589
00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:50.860
Yeah, but I didn't think they knew that Dwight and Angela were going to get together.

590
00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:51.520
They just were.

591
00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:56.560
I don't know where that storyline came from, but I imagine they were just kind of floundering in season two.

592
00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:57.860
Like, oh, we got to mix things up.

593
00:24:57.860 --> 00:25:01.300
Like, because at first they had me together with Kelly.

594
00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:03.000
They had me kiss Kelly Kapoor.

595
00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:05.120
But Angela was furious.

596
00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:07.780
Angela watched it and was furious.

597
00:25:09.260 --> 00:25:14.900
Remember, they had me like look in the camera like they made me look so like they kept saying more anger, more.

598
00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:17.300
I was like, oh, my God, my eyes are going to pop out of my head.

599
00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:21.000
But I think I think our writers just sat there.

600
00:25:21.100 --> 00:25:23.620
Jenna and I have talked about this and we've talked to our writers about it,

601
00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:32.900
how they would just take two names and put them on a card and just see what that felt like and sort of spitball a little bit about what those two people would bring out of each other.

602
00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:33.800
Right.

603
00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:37.360
And that is one thing that Greg talked about.

604
00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:41.660
And I remember, and it's really great lesson for comedy writers and screenwriters.

605
00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:49.860
He said a lot of shows really focus on a character and like, oh, let's make this character really specific and funny and interesting.

606
00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:55.960
But he was really about like when these two two characters bang up against each other.

607
00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:57.600
That's what she said.

608
00:25:57.860 --> 00:25:59.340
Then what do what happens?

609
00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:02.040
What happens with that energy?

610
00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:06.400
You know, so it was more about he was much more about dynamics between characters.

611
00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:09.060
And I think that's one of the successes of the office.

612
00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:17.980
Well, this episode that we're talking about today, Golden Ticket, has a classic Michael Dwight pairing.

613
00:26:18.940 --> 00:26:29.720
And we just I love in this rewatch watching the way that Dwight can be both Michael's lackey and then also like you can take him to task.

614
00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:30.880
You stand up to him.

615
00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:33.600
What was that like for you?

616
00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:39.820
You were in this comedy duo with Steve Carell for seven years, playing out moments like this.

617
00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:41.560
What was that experience?

618
00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:45.460
Well, that's a bunch of questions.

619
00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:49.020
And I know I that's how I like to interview people.

620
00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:56.040
She just likes to throw like 13 big, overarching, like huge questions all at once.

621
00:26:56.540 --> 00:26:57.360
I love it.

622
00:26:57.560 --> 00:26:58.780
Well, let's let me hit that one.

623
00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:13.420
Let me just say that, you know, when I look back on that work with Steve, I'm just so grateful because he's obviously like one of the greateSt.

624
00:27:14.660 --> 00:27:18.660
Actors, comedy actors, but even just actors like ever.

625
00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:24.660
And the fact that I got to do so much work with him was just such a gift.

626
00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:27.380
I didn't come from an improv background.

627
00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:33.520
I was always good at improv, but I went to theater school and I did Shakespeare and I did Eugene O'Neill.

628
00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:38.220
And I was in New York kind of off off Broadway theater guy.

629
00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:41.980
And Steve was from Second City and had done years and years and years of improv.

630
00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:44.440
So that was his thing.

631
00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:49.500
But the thing about Steve that, you know, they always have this.

632
00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:51.380
It's kind of become a cliche, like the yes.

633
00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:53.320
And, you know, like an improv.

634
00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:59.680
If you say like, hey, you know, I bought a pet peacock.

635
00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:03.380
Then you say yes.

636
00:28:03.580 --> 00:28:05.680
And like, oh, I'd love to see it.

637
00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:07.460
Can I feed it my mealworms or whatever?

638
00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:10.340
And Steve was incredible at that.

639
00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:11.620
No matter what I did.

640
00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:15.520
And sometimes I would try to go really out of the box.

641
00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:24.080
Steve would completely react in character and accept what was being proposed.

642
00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:28.320
So I could just in the because obviously we would we would shoot a scene.

643
00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:29.760
We would improvise.

644
00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:31.740
I mean, we would do the script.

645
00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:34.460
We would shoot the script and make sure we got it scripted correctly.

646
00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:36.380
And then we would could improvise.

647
00:28:36.380 --> 00:28:40.040
And then sometimes it would just we just freely improvise and just go off.

648
00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:48.500
And I remember just being like I could say something like I decided to open a llama farm just out of nowhere or something.

649
00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:53.680
And Steve would just listen as Michael take that in and be like, wait, what are you talking about?

650
00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:54.580
Llamas?

651
00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:56.760
What what does that have to do with anything?

652
00:28:57.100 --> 00:28:57.440
Why?

653
00:28:58.180 --> 00:28:59.700
You're so weird, Dwight.

654
00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:00.680
Oh, go away.

655
00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:01.140
You're icky.

656
00:29:01.580 --> 00:29:04.600
Like and it would just be completely in the moment.

657
00:29:04.600 --> 00:29:09.200
And I just learned so much from that process.

658
00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:13.840
He never got kind of like, God damn it, Rain.

659
00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:15.980
Why are you being so weird and so silly?

660
00:29:16.180 --> 00:29:17.720
Like stay on the point of the scene.

661
00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:23.460
And we can go way off kilter, you know, way off the path.

662
00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:26.920
And he would just be right there all the time.

663
00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:30.060
It was really a privilege to work with him.

664
00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:37.840
I loved it when there'd be a conference room scene and clearly Michael had an agenda and things to say.

665
00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:41.640
And you you as Dwight, of all people, would interject.

666
00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:42.200
Right.

667
00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:49.900
And Rain, so many times I go to the script to see if it was written or improvised because I feel like I can tell now.

668
00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:52.780
And they're always so good.

669
00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:54.840
But but it's like seamless.

670
00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:58.920
You would never know because of exactly what you just shared, because Steve is Michael.

671
00:29:59.140 --> 00:30:03.820
No matter what you piped up and said, just like would just take it and run with it.

672
00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:08.000
And it was always like an absolute joy.

673
00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:08.620
You know me.

674
00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:10.500
I'm a nerdy, nerdy improv gal.

675
00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:15.120
Well, we've talked on the podcast that you and Jenna had your whole like we did theater.

676
00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:19.320
And Jenna likes to remind me of it, Rain, much like you did.

677
00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:26.540
But as like a nerdy improv gal watching you guys, it was it was just awesome.

678
00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:30.060
I felt like I had a front row seat of just some really magical moments.

679
00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:32.940
And getting specifically to Golden Ticket.

680
00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:33.900
Thank you, Angela.

681
00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:38.680
And but yeah, I hadn't seen this one since it came out.

682
00:30:38.680 --> 00:30:42.480
And it was so fun to watch.

683
00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:53.860
And Jenna, just like you said, that dynamic of Dwight being toady, lackey, suck up, just so desperate for Michael's approval.

684
00:30:55.260 --> 00:30:59.320
And then oddly, realizing he's got some power here.

685
00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:08.460
And, you know, that switch when he's shaking David Wallace's hand and he says, you know, thank you and takes the credit for it.

686
00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:15.180
And then Steve's expression of it was so priceless of like, oh, that guy.

687
00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:23.880
And then your fight in the marketing meeting, like when you get on the phone with the marketing guys, the two of you back and forth with your big ideas.

688
00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:33.360
Yeah, horse boats and all the toilet, toilet, toilet net is a lot of toilet ideas.

689
00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:35.380
You don't have ideas.

690
00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:36.100
Yes, I do.

691
00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:44.460
So I would love to see an extended cut of that because I bet that there were a lot more ideas we tossed back and forth in that scene.

692
00:31:44.780 --> 00:31:50.600
We should ask Dave Rogers, the keeper of all of the extra footage, because I bet you're right.

693
00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:52.140
There had to have been even more.

694
00:31:52.140 --> 00:32:02.300
Also, this one was written by Mindy Kaling, and she was amazing at coming up with on the fly, in the moment, extra stuff to hand you.

695
00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:08.600
I have to tell you guys, in the script, I reread it, Pam tries to butt in with an idea.

696
00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:09.300
What?

697
00:32:09.640 --> 00:32:10.120
Yes.

698
00:32:10.340 --> 00:32:13.880
And Michael and Dwight shoot her down.

699
00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:17.340
It's the one thing they agree on is that Pam's idea is so stupid.

700
00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:23.600
She says, as they're yelling all their ideas, Pam meekly just says, remote control toilets.

701
00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:25.520
And they're both like, nah, it's stupid.

702
00:32:25.620 --> 00:32:26.220
Doesn't make sense.

703
00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:26.700
It's dumb.

704
00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:29.600
That's great.

705
00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:30.740
I wish they would have kept that.

706
00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:31.360
That's great.

707
00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:34.920
We have another question that we've never asked you, Rain.

708
00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:38.420
This is a popular one we ask all our guests.

709
00:32:38.980 --> 00:32:39.200
Okay.

710
00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:42.140
Did you take anything from set when the show wrapped?

711
00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:45.160
Oh, this breaks my heart.

712
00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:48.200
Um, I should have taken so much more.

713
00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:50.380
I should have fleeced NBC Universal.

714
00:32:51.020 --> 00:32:53.820
Um, I took Dwight's glasses.

715
00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:57.360
I took his little desk plate that said Dwight Schrute on it.

716
00:32:57.800 --> 00:32:59.220
I took his stapler.

717
00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:04.980
And one or two other things I can't quite remember.

718
00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:06.460
I have to look around and see.

719
00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:10.520
But I had a whole list of things I gave to Phil Shea.

720
00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:13.340
And I said, Phil, pull these aside for me.

721
00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:15.400
And I'll get them from you later.

722
00:33:16.140 --> 00:33:21.840
And it was, you know, Dwight's briefcase and this and that and the other thing and blah,

723
00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:22.160
blah, blah.

724
00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:25.100
And, um, and Phil was like, okay.

725
00:33:25.240 --> 00:33:31.410
And then I went back to Phil and he's like, I couldn't get them.

726
00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:32.390
Sorry, blah, blah, blah.

727
00:33:32.570 --> 00:33:33.970
And then I heard from someone else.

728
00:33:34.130 --> 00:33:39.990
They were like, oh, they threatened the crew to be fired and never worked for NBC Universal again.

729
00:33:39.990 --> 00:33:45.610
If they caught them taking anything from the office.

730
00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:48.570
And we didn't know why.

731
00:33:48.850 --> 00:33:54.070
And then they had that big auction where they auctioned all this stuff off on eBay.

732
00:33:54.610 --> 00:33:57.310
You know, even like Dwight's desk was auctioned off.

733
00:33:57.510 --> 00:33:59.470
Did you not get your bobblehead?

734
00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:06.010
I got one of the rejected bobbleheads that doesn't look anything like the bobblehead.

735
00:34:06.110 --> 00:34:07.770
I did not get my original bobblehead.

736
00:34:07.990 --> 00:34:08.070
No.

737
00:34:08.350 --> 00:34:08.890
I see.

738
00:34:09.510 --> 00:34:10.150
That's a shame.

739
00:34:10.150 --> 00:34:14.110
And I have to feel shame for the painting, the Dwight painting that's kind of where he's

740
00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:15.010
like Chairman Mao.

741
00:34:15.330 --> 00:34:15.490
Yeah, yeah.

742
00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:18.970
And, um, that didn't happen either.

743
00:34:19.570 --> 00:34:23.550
Well, Rain, you know, you've gone on to do so many awesome things, but we would love to

744
00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:27.130
talk to you about your podcast, Metaphysical Milkshake.

745
00:34:27.350 --> 00:34:32.170
I listened to a great episode last night when, um, you had Mike Schur on.

746
00:34:32.970 --> 00:34:33.410
Yeah.

747
00:34:33.910 --> 00:34:35.570
And that was so amazing.

748
00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:37.830
Just talking about what is good, what is bad.

749
00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:41.310
And he sort of like folded in his show, The Good Place into that episode.

750
00:34:41.310 --> 00:34:46.430
But you and Reza are so thoughtful and you're really asking life's big questions.

751
00:34:46.430 --> 00:34:48.270
And it's a great listen.

752
00:34:49.790 --> 00:34:50.470
Yeah, thanks.

753
00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:54.350
Well, you guys know this is something that's always been really important to me.

754
00:34:54.530 --> 00:35:00.610
And it was kind of the impetus and inspiration for starting Soul Pancake, a digital media company

755
00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:04.610
that, um, we, we, I created with some friends.

756
00:35:04.610 --> 00:35:09.630
Um, but this whole idea of life's biggest possible questions has always really intrigued me.

